00:00:06.678 --> 00:00:08.220
MARTIN GORNER: So
who in the audience

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:13.080
would describe themselves
as developers or computer

00:00:13.080 --> 00:00:18.470
engineers with a new skill
set in machine learning?

00:00:18.470 --> 00:00:19.540
Quite a few.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:21.000
So that's a good thing.

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:23.600
And I would like to share
with you a couple of tools

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:27.140
that you can use day to
day to work faster and more

00:00:27.140 --> 00:00:28.080
efficiently.

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:30.650
TPUs is, of course,
one of them, but there

00:00:30.650 --> 00:00:32.250
will be a couple of others.

00:00:32.250 --> 00:00:34.220
So this is a TPU v2.

00:00:34.220 --> 00:00:36.260
This is a TPU v3.

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:37.850
Those are fairly large boards.

00:00:37.850 --> 00:00:39.830
It's like this, roughly.

00:00:39.830 --> 00:00:42.140
And each one of those
boards has four chips.

00:00:42.140 --> 00:00:44.480
Each chip is a dual core chip.

00:00:44.480 --> 00:00:48.830
And those accelerators
are designed specifically

00:00:48.830 --> 00:00:52.100
to accelerate machine
learning workloads.

00:00:52.100 --> 00:00:54.890
You can get them as
boards like this.

00:00:54.890 --> 00:00:57.770
You can also get
them as a full rack.

00:00:57.770 --> 00:00:59.270
It's called a pod.

00:00:59.270 --> 00:01:02.540
And the trick is that
in a rack, all the TPUs

00:01:02.540 --> 00:01:05.660
are interconnected on a high
performance interconnect which

00:01:05.660 --> 00:01:09.750
makes them look like a single
accelerator to your workload.

00:01:09.750 --> 00:01:12.650
So you don't have to handle
complicated distribution code.

00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:16.190
The only thing you do is
you increase your batch size

00:01:16.190 --> 00:01:19.550
to work on those
bigger machines.

00:01:19.550 --> 00:01:24.220
And just a couple of words
of what makes them special.

00:01:24.220 --> 00:01:25.510
Inside of a TPU--

00:01:25.510 --> 00:01:29.080
so a board, four
chips, eight cores--

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:31.850
in each one of those
cores, you have two things.

00:01:31.850 --> 00:01:36.580
One is a fairly traditional
vector processing unit--

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:39.172
so that's a vector processor--
and the second one,

00:01:39.172 --> 00:01:41.380
that is what makes this
special for machine learning,

00:01:41.380 --> 00:01:43.360
is a matrix multiply unit.

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:46.120
So it's a specific unit--

00:01:46.120 --> 00:01:49.720
hardware unit-- that is
dedicated to performing 128

00:01:49.720 --> 00:01:55.420
by 128 matrix multiplications
in reduced precision in a format

00:01:55.420 --> 00:01:57.880
called bfloat 16.

00:01:57.880 --> 00:02:00.670
And machine learning
workloads are

00:02:00.670 --> 00:02:03.330
full of matrix multiplications.

00:02:03.330 --> 00:02:07.120
So by having this dedicated
hardware in there,

00:02:07.120 --> 00:02:09.789
this is what accelerates
machine learning workload,

00:02:09.789 --> 00:02:12.310
specifically.

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:16.150
Now, about this precision,
so using reduced precision

00:02:16.150 --> 00:02:21.190
is a fairly common trick
in machine learning.

00:02:21.190 --> 00:02:27.240
Usually, you do so in float16,
the standard float16 precision.

00:02:27.240 --> 00:02:31.660
But as you see here, float16
has only five exponent bits,

00:02:31.660 --> 00:02:34.840
so fewer than
float32, which means

00:02:34.840 --> 00:02:36.760
that when you reduce
the precision,

00:02:36.760 --> 00:02:39.940
sometimes you get under
flows or overflows

00:02:39.940 --> 00:02:42.250
and you have to do
additional work on your model

00:02:42.250 --> 00:02:43.510
to overcome that.

00:02:43.510 --> 00:02:46.760
It's not necessarily
complicated, but it's work.

00:02:46.760 --> 00:02:49.210
And so what we did
in TPUs is that we

00:02:49.210 --> 00:02:53.920
used a slightly different
16-bit format called bfloat16,

00:02:53.920 --> 00:02:57.880
and you see it has exactly the
same number of exponent bits

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:02.500
than float32, just the end is
cutoff, so reduced precision,

00:03:02.500 --> 00:03:04.310
but exactly the same range.

00:03:04.310 --> 00:03:08.770
So usually, bfloat16 is a
drop-in replacement for float32

00:03:08.770 --> 00:03:12.370
in neural network workloads.

00:03:12.370 --> 00:03:14.880
And also, we do the
multiplications--

00:03:14.880 --> 00:03:18.100
16-bit multiplied by 16-bit,
which gives you a 32-bit

00:03:18.100 --> 00:03:24.540
result, and we keep this
32-bit result. And finally,

00:03:24.540 --> 00:03:28.870
the way it's architected
is that we have--

00:03:28.870 --> 00:03:32.120
so try to remember from
your calculus courses

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:34.650
how to perform a
matrix multiplication.

00:03:34.650 --> 00:03:38.120
Each result is a dot product
of one line of one matrix

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:40.520
and one column of
the second matrix.

00:03:40.520 --> 00:03:42.780
And a dot product is
a series of multiply

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:44.420
and accumulate operations.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:47.270
Multiply, plus, multiply,
plus, multiply, plus.

00:03:47.270 --> 00:03:50.390
So that's why the unit
component of this array

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:52.550
is a multiply accumulator.

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:57.200
And by having 128 by 128
multiply accumulators connected

00:03:57.200 --> 00:04:01.070
like this, I am not
expecting you to understand

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:03.020
this animation immediately.

00:04:03.020 --> 00:04:06.000
You will have to spend a little
bit more time on it, I guess.

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:09.980
But the idea is that you load
one matrix into the array.

00:04:09.980 --> 00:04:13.430
The other matrix, the values,
they flow through the arrays.

00:04:13.430 --> 00:04:15.500
All the intermediate
sums, they flow

00:04:15.500 --> 00:04:17.459
through the array
in this direction,

00:04:17.459 --> 00:04:22.670
and what comes out from this
end are your final dot products

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:24.830
of the resulting matrix.

00:04:24.830 --> 00:04:26.570
So it's a one pass operation.

00:04:26.570 --> 00:04:29.030
No memory access while
you're doing this.

00:04:29.030 --> 00:04:31.550
No writing to register
as an intermediate sum

00:04:31.550 --> 00:04:34.670
and reading it back,
which is why it's fast.

00:04:34.670 --> 00:04:40.310
And the second advantage is
that the unit of processing--

00:04:40.310 --> 00:04:44.560
the 16-bit by 16-bit to
32-bit multiply accumulate--

00:04:44.560 --> 00:04:46.680
it's a tiny piece of hardware.

00:04:46.680 --> 00:04:48.780
It's not a full processor
that can do anything.

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:50.690
It's just a multiply
accumulator,

00:04:50.690 --> 00:04:54.160
and that's why we can
cram a huge number of them

00:04:54.160 --> 00:04:55.280
in each TPU.

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:58.160
Each TPU core, 128 by 128--

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:02.000
16,000 of those multiply
cumulative compute units,

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:07.560
and, of course, a TPU is dual
core, so 32,000 in total.

00:05:07.560 --> 00:05:10.340
And this is the architecture--
so specifically for matrix

00:05:10.340 --> 00:05:11.720
multiplications--

00:05:11.720 --> 00:05:14.510
that gives TPUs an edge
in terms of density.

00:05:14.510 --> 00:05:18.260
Density means power,
and power means cost.

00:05:18.260 --> 00:05:21.500
A rough idea of the cost--

00:05:21.500 --> 00:05:24.080
some people might have
seen my talk from last year

00:05:24.080 --> 00:05:27.530
about building this model,
and I'm using this model

00:05:27.530 --> 00:05:30.560
as a benchmark these days.

00:05:30.560 --> 00:05:35.770
So this model trains in four
and a half hours on a GPU,

00:05:35.770 --> 00:05:38.770
and if I provision
five GPUs in a cluster,

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:41.210
I can train it in one hour.

00:05:41.210 --> 00:05:44.030
And I chose this example because
I can train it in one hour

00:05:44.030 --> 00:05:46.670
also on a TPU v2.

00:05:46.670 --> 00:05:51.110
So the rough idea is
this four-chip board

00:05:51.110 --> 00:05:53.840
is roughly equivalent to
five of the biggest GPUs

00:05:53.840 --> 00:05:56.900
that we have in terms of
speed, and in terms of cost,

00:05:56.900 --> 00:06:01.230
is roughly three times cheaper.

