My code is running
on a VM, a normal VM.

00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:14.630
The TPU is attached
through the network to it.

00:14:14.630 --> 00:14:19.350
TensorFlow defines my model
as a computation graph,

00:14:19.350 --> 00:14:22.880
and it's that computation
graph that is shipped off

00:14:22.880 --> 00:14:26.060
to the remote TPU for training.

00:14:26.060 --> 00:14:28.940
And from that
computation graph, we

00:14:28.940 --> 00:14:33.230
use XLA, the accelerated
linear algebra compiler,

00:14:33.230 --> 00:14:37.040
to compile that graph
into TPU micro code.

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:40.100
So the TPU never sees
your Python code.

00:14:40.100 --> 00:14:41.640
You have to bear that in mind.

00:14:41.640 --> 00:14:43.890
It only sees your graph.

00:14:43.890 --> 00:14:48.230
But if you use the
tf.data.Dataset API,

00:14:48.230 --> 00:14:51.370
your data pipeline is also
packaged as part of this graph,

00:14:51.370 --> 00:14:53.840
so the TPU will be
pulling data directly

00:14:53.840 --> 00:14:55.980
from GCS, which
is very efficient.

00:14:55.980 --> 00:14:57.480
And as you can see here--

00:14:57.480 --> 00:14:59.390
this is what I love about TPUs--

00:14:59.390 --> 00:15:02.180
on small to medium models,
I can get training times

00:15:02.180 --> 00:15:06.080
of four seconds per epoch,
and I can iterate in minutes,

00:15:06.080 --> 00:15:10.140
interactively, in a
notebook, on various models.

00:15:10.140 --> 00:15:11.180
This is a smaller model.

00:15:11.180 --> 00:15:13.370
This one has 3,000 images.

00:15:13.370 --> 00:15:16.920
But you see, it's
already trained.

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:18.720
15 epochs.

00:15:18.720 --> 00:15:22.880
We will see if it trained well.

00:15:22.880 --> 00:15:23.380
Yep.

00:15:23.380 --> 00:15:25.145
Here is the accuracy--

00:15:25.145 --> 00:15:27.940
fine, it go to 75%.

00:15:27.940 --> 00:15:32.310
And let us run a couple
of predictions from it,

00:15:32.310 --> 00:15:36.960
see if it is classifying
my flowers correctly.

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:37.710
Running.

00:15:37.710 --> 00:15:38.820
Displaying.

00:15:38.820 --> 00:15:40.350
Here we go.

00:15:40.350 --> 00:15:42.150
So it doesn't get
all of them right,

00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:44.910
but the dandelion is fine--
the roses, the sunflowers,

00:15:44.910 --> 00:15:46.140
dandelion.

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:49.847
And it mistook-- well,
this is a hard one, right?

00:15:49.847 --> 00:15:51.180
You've got a couple of mistakes.

00:15:51.180 --> 00:15:52.470
No model is perfect.

00:15:52.470 --> 00:15:54.740
But this is what
I love about TPUs.

00:15:54.740 --> 00:15:58.780
It's really fast, and you
can use them interactively.

00:15:58.780 --> 00:16:04.050
But now, I want to show
you a bigger model.

00:16:04.050 --> 00:16:05.140
A bigger one.

00:16:05.140 --> 00:16:08.080
And for this, of course,
a bigger model that you

00:16:08.080 --> 00:16:11.040
will be training
in half an hour,

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:14.160
that is not something that
you would be comfortable

00:16:14.160 --> 00:16:15.960
doing in your notebook--

00:16:15.960 --> 00:16:18.030
I mean, doing
interactively on a machine.

00:16:18.030 --> 00:16:20.790
You don't want to be paying
for an expensive machine

00:16:20.790 --> 00:16:22.740
to type code.

00:16:22.740 --> 00:16:24.900
That is only when you
do interactive work.

00:16:24.900 --> 00:16:27.990
I want something that
allows me to take a notebook

00:16:27.990 --> 00:16:31.950
and launch it as a
job on hardware that

00:16:31.950 --> 00:16:33.750
will be provisioned
just for that job

00:16:33.750 --> 00:16:36.720
and decommissioned when
the job is finished.

00:16:36.720 --> 00:16:41.560
That is the Fairing API.

00:16:41.560 --> 00:16:43.620
So that's a new thing.

00:16:43.620 --> 00:16:48.090
Reminder-- the three lines to
get a Keras model on a TPU.

00:16:48.090 --> 00:16:51.990
The couple of lines to
load a tfrecord data

00:16:51.990 --> 00:16:55.260
set, using interleave
to read from 16

00:16:55.260 --> 00:16:58.420
different files at once.

00:16:58.420 --> 00:17:00.780
But the last thing
that I want to show you

00:17:00.780 --> 00:17:03.560
is a new API called Fairing.

00:17:03.560 --> 00:17:06.829
So let me show you the code.

00:17:06.829 --> 00:17:10.010
I'm back into my previous
notebook with the detection

00:17:10.010 --> 00:17:14.339
model, and now, the
Fairing API is just

00:17:14.339 --> 00:17:16.079
this-- it has three lines.

00:17:16.079 --> 00:17:20.400
First, set builder to say
something about my environment.

00:17:20.400 --> 00:17:22.050
I have actually
built my dependencies

00:17:22.050 --> 00:17:23.040
as a Docker container.

00:17:23.040 --> 00:17:24.240
That's a couple of lines.

00:17:24.240 --> 00:17:27.060
I just have a couple
of [INAUDIBLE]..

00:17:27.060 --> 00:17:30.300
The second one, which says
where are you deploying?

00:17:30.300 --> 00:17:33.150
I'm deploying to GCP,
which is ML Engine.

00:17:33.150 --> 00:17:36.090
And this is the hardware
configuration I want.

00:17:36.090 --> 00:17:39.450
I actually want a Cloud TPU v3
to be provisioned for this job

00:17:39.450 --> 00:17:41.830
and destroyed after the job.

00:17:41.830 --> 00:17:45.180
And finally, what
am I deploying?

00:17:45.180 --> 00:17:47.080
I'm deploying a full notebook.

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:49.480
This is a notebook-friendly API.

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:53.190
I have the name of
my notebook, and I

00:17:53.190 --> 00:17:58.260
have a couple of sidecar files,
which I listed behind it.

00:17:58.260 --> 00:18:04.100
Now, when I launch this, it
will build all of those things

00:18:04.100 --> 00:18:06.240
into another Docker container.

00:18:06.240 --> 00:18:08.150
I don't have to
know about Docker.

00:18:08.150 --> 00:18:09.950
It does it.

00:18:09.950 --> 00:18:15.500
And launch a job on ML Engine,
which I think we will see here.

00:18:15.500 --> 00:18:19.540
Let us go to Jobs on
ML Engine, and write.

00:18:19.540 --> 00:18:20.740
I have two jobs running.

00:18:20.740 --> 00:18:23.900
This is a new one has been
